1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,085
When you watched the inbox triage agent work in Section 0, the box doing the actual

2
00:00:05,085 --> 00:00:07,709
reading was Google's Gemini, on its free tier.

3
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:10,219
That was not an arbitrary choice for a demo.

4
00:00:10,219 --> 00:00:14,954
It is genuinely the easiest way into this course, and Priya starts here too, before

5
00:00:14,954 --> 00:00:17,692
her volume forces an upgrade later in Section 9.

6
00:00:17,692 --> 00:00:20,088
What is a model, stripped of the mystique?

7
00:00:20,088 --> 00:00:23,796
A service on the internet that takes text in and gives text back.

8
00:00:23,796 --> 00:00:28,189
Your workflow sends it an email plus your plain English rules; it returns its

9
00:00:28,189 --> 00:00:28,645
reading.

10
00:00:28,645 --> 00:00:31,554
That is the entire transaction, repeated every run.

11
00:00:31,554 --> 00:00:32,981
Two useful things follow.

12
00:00:32,981 --> 00:00:37,658
The providers are largely interchangeable from n8n's point of view: the model sits

13
00:00:37,658 --> 00:00:42,051
in its own box, so swapping Gemini for another provider later is a two minute

14
00:00:42,051 --> 00:00:43,306
change, not a rebuild.

15
00:00:43,306 --> 00:00:47,927
And because the models improve every few months on their own, your agents quietly

16
00:00:47,927 --> 00:00:50,266
get better without you touching anything.

17
00:00:50,266 --> 00:00:55,058
You are renting judgement by the call, from a market that keeps cutting the price of

18
00:00:55,058 --> 00:00:55,229
it.

19
00:00:55,779 --> 00:00:57,267
Read that middle row again.

20
00:00:57,267 --> 00:01:01,844
Gemini's free tier is not a trial that runs out, it is a genuinely free tier with a

21
00:01:01,844 --> 00:01:03,498
rate limit instead of a clock.

22
00:01:03,498 --> 00:01:07,910
For one person automating their own inbox, that rate limit will not be the thing

23
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:08,737
that stops you.

24
00:01:08,737 --> 00:01:11,659
That is why this course builds every demo against it.

25
00:01:11,659 --> 00:01:15,244
Now the cost that is not on the table, because it is not a price.

26
00:01:15,244 --> 00:01:19,765
Free tiers are free because the provider gets something: on some of them, what you

27
00:01:19,765 --> 00:01:24,176
send can be used to improve the service, under terms that change more often than

28
00:01:24,176 --> 00:01:25,445
this course re-renders.

29
00:01:25,445 --> 00:01:29,580
That is a fine trade for a practice inbox and a terrible default for client

30
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:30,407
correspondence.

31
00:01:30,407 --> 00:01:34,598
So the working rule while you learn is the one Priya follows: free tier plus

32
00:01:34,598 --> 00:01:38,899
practice data until you have read your provider's current data terms with work

33
00:01:38,899 --> 00:01:40,498
content specifically in mind.

34
00:01:40,498 --> 00:01:44,909
When the agents go live on real client email in Section 3, that reading is a ten

35
00:01:44,909 --> 00:01:49,266
minute task you do once, and it belongs to you, not to this course, because the

36
00:01:49,266 --> 00:01:52,023
terms will be whatever they are the week you look.

37
00:01:52,573 --> 00:01:57,035
Section 9 puts real numbers against this, but the shape of the decision is simple.

38
00:01:57,035 --> 00:01:59,321
Free tiers are rate limited, not crippled.

39
00:01:59,321 --> 00:02:03,892
You will feel the ceiling as a slowdown, not a bill, and by the time you feel it you

40
00:02:03,892 --> 00:02:08,463
will already know exactly how much of your week the agent is worth, because you will

41
00:02:08,463 --> 00:02:10,585
have been running it for weeks by then.

42
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:15,157
It is worth knowing what the ceiling feels like from inside n8n, so it does not read

43
00:02:15,157 --> 00:02:16,626
as breakage the first time.

44
00:02:16,626 --> 00:02:20,272
The model's box goes red, and the message mentions limits or quota.

45
00:02:20,272 --> 00:02:21,197
Nothing is wrong.

46
00:02:21,197 --> 00:02:25,605
The provider is saying not this minute, and a minute later the same run succeeds.

47
00:02:25,605 --> 00:02:29,959
If you see it more than occasionally, that is your volume talking, and it is the

48
00:02:29,959 --> 00:02:34,530
most pleasant upgrade prompt in software: it means the agent is being used enough to

49
00:02:34,530 --> 00:02:34,911
matter.

50
00:02:34,911 --> 00:02:39,428
One more choice hides inside each provider: models come in families, small and fast

51
00:02:39,428 --> 00:02:40,679
against large and deep.

52
00:02:40,679 --> 00:02:45,087
Triage, extraction and sorting, which is most of office work, belong to the small

53
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:49,550
fast ones, and every build in this course names the model tier it uses for exactly

54
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:50,203
that reason.

55
00:02:50,203 --> 00:02:54,774
Reaching for the biggest model for a sorting job is paying for a surgeon to open the

56
00:02:54,774 --> 00:02:55,046
post.

57
00:02:55,596 --> 00:02:59,870
Getting the key itself is the one step this course cannot walk you through on

58
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:03,700
screen, because it means logging into an account only you can create.

59
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:08,140
It takes about two minutes on Google's side, and once it is pasted into n8n, you

60
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:11,471
will not need to touch it again for the rest of this course.

61
00:03:11,471 --> 00:03:15,800
Two small habits while you are on the provider's dashboard, both thirty second

62
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:16,466
investments.

63
00:03:16,466 --> 00:03:20,962
Name the key for what it does, something like n8n dash agents, because six months

64
00:03:20,962 --> 00:03:25,569
from now a list of unnamed keys is a list of things you are afraid to delete, and a

65
00:03:25,569 --> 00:03:29,233
named key can be revoked surgically the day a laptop goes missing.

66
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:33,729
And make one key per purpose rather than reusing one everywhere: if this key ever

67
00:03:33,729 --> 00:03:38,391
needs cutting, you want to cut the agents, not everything else you built on the same

68
00:03:38,391 --> 00:03:38,836
account.

69
00:03:38,836 --> 00:03:40,778
Neither habit costs anything today.

70
00:03:40,778 --> 00:03:44,331
Both are the kind of thing Section 8 will make you glad you did.

71
00:03:44,881 --> 00:03:49,644
With both halves of the toolbox connected, n8n and a model, you are ready for the

72
00:03:49,644 --> 00:03:50,467
canvas itself.

73
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:55,113
Next lecture: reading what you are looking at, so Section 3's build is familiar

74
00:03:55,113 --> 00:03:55,995
instead of new.
